WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.148
Delivery Operations wants to know: of all parcels

00:03.148 --> 00:05.468
due by noon, how many arrived on time?

00:07.207 --> 00:09.891
At one o’clock, two parcels arrived on time,

00:09.891 --> 00:13.026
one arrived late, and one is unfinished. All

00:13.026 --> 00:15.608
four were due by noon. Assume these records

00:15.608 --> 00:16.219
are complete.

00:17.944 --> 00:21.421
First, ask about completed deliveries. The late parcel

00:21.421 --> 00:24.966
belongs because it arrived. The unfinished parcel stays

00:24.966 --> 00:27.441
outside this group. Two on time out of

00:27.441 --> 00:30.540
three completed gives sixty-six point seven percent.

00:32.265 --> 00:35.769
Now answer the operations question. Which parcels belong

00:35.769 --> 00:38.815
in its denominator? Pause and explain your choice.

00:44.094 --> 00:47.049
All four were due, including the unfinished parcel.

00:47.049 --> 00:49.574
The numerator stays at two. Two out of

00:49.574 --> 00:50.842
four gives fifty percent.

00:52.557 --> 00:55.613
Try a separate example with complete records: six

00:55.613 --> 00:58.177
parcels due, two on time, two late and

00:58.177 --> 01:01.426
two unfinished. What percentage of all parcels due

01:01.426 --> 01:03.993
arrived on time? Pause before continuing.

01:09.306 --> 01:11.904
Two out of six gives thirty-three point three

01:11.904 --> 01:15.530
percent. Dividing by four would answer the completed-delivery

01:15.530 --> 01:16.551
question instead.

01:18.324 --> 01:22.383
Record the population, measurement time and responsible team.

01:22.383 --> 01:26.183
Then check the calculation against that definition. A

01:26.183 --> 01:28.962
successful query can still answer a different question.
